{"custom_id": "cc08d85273e7b0e683517e0989fe9ebc8bcad721522e9ad2", "meta": {"loop": "topk4L04", "task": "infer_discrepancy_patterns_1", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "max_tokens": 128000, "prompt_sha256": "49442c345056f22bcd1bb0f66574f5c61a086bb2de8ae1605b745eaac6a7880c", "prompt_chars": 10867}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_017vuPQBMXMbU5QJdLT6sNog", "saved_utc": "2026-10-23T10:36:11+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011CgK1jYD3rbqmFXX757u98", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "I'm checking why \"unilateral retinal telangiectasis,\" which appears as a synonym for Coats' disease, was marked DiseaseClass by the LLM despite being gold-labeled SpecificDisease—likely because the generic term \"telangiectasis\" made it look like a descriptive pathology class rather than a specific named disease.\n\nFor \"fever,\" the gold label is oddly SpecificDisease even though it's really a symptom, suggesting possible annotation inconsistency rather than a true LLM error. Similarly, \"Chronic neisserial infection\" is gold-labeled SpecificDisease, but it reads more like a disease class caused by the Neisseria genus, so I suspect the same kind of annotation noise is at play here too.\n\nThe pattern I'm noticing is that the model consistently treats \"qualifier + generic pathology noun\" constructions like \"cutaneous vasculitis,\" \"chronic neisserial infection,\" and \"retinal telangiectasis\" as DiseaseClass because they read like category descriptions, while gold annotations treat these same phrases as SpecificDisease entities.\n\nLooking more broadly, it seems DiseaseClass in this schema tends to capture phenotypic or symptom-level categories (like \"mental retardation\" or \"exudative retinal detachment,\" which are manifestations rather than named diseases), while SpecificDisease covers concrete nosological entities like Coats disease or fragile X syndrome. I also notice a separate contrast where disease names used as noun modifiers—like \"ovarian cancer\" in \"family history of ovarian cancer\"—get tagged as Modifier instead.\n\nActually \"mental retardation\" being DiseaseClass despite its generic head is consistent with the model treating it as a phenotype category, so that's not really a counterexample. What seems to distinguish the discrepancies is whether the span is introduced as the specific disease under discussion in context—whether through an alias/synonym appositive, or as a clinical diagnosis embedded in the patient's presentation—versus a purely generic descriptive category.\n\nThe common trigger seems to be a generic pathophysiological head noun (telangiectasis, vasculitis, infection, fever) modified by an anatomical or descriptive term (retinal, cutaneous, unilateral, chronic neisserial), making the model read it as a class label rather than a specific named disease, even when the document treats it as a distinct nosological entity. Phrases like \"so-called\" or parenthetical naming (\"(unilateral retinal telangiectasis)\" following \"Coats' disease\") are strong alias signals that the gold annotation respects but the model may still parse differently.\n\nBut checking true positives complicates this: \"ovarian cancer\" has a generic head noun too but is correctly labeled as Modifier rather than DiseaseClass, so a simple head-noun generic/eponym distinction doesn't cleanly separate the discrepancy cases from the correct ones.\n\nLooking at the actual DiseaseClass examples — \"mental retardation\" and \"exudative retinal detachment\" are symptoms/manifestations listed as disease features, which fits. But \"fever\" is also a symptom yet gold labels it SpecificDisease, so the gold standard itself seems inconsistent, and \"cutaneous vasculitis\" sits ambiguously between manifestation and disease category too. It seems the model is treating \"retinal telangiectasis\" similarly, as a manifestation/abnormal finding rather than a named disease.\n\nThe cleaner pattern is that the model defaults to DiseaseClass for compositional adjective+pathology-noun phrases, but gold marks SpecificDisease when that same phrase functions as the actual referential name for a particular disease — like when it's introduced with \"so-called,\" restated parenthetically, or used as a diagnostic label in a case report rather than a generic descriptive term.\n\nI also notice some gold labels, like \"other causes of cutaneous vasculitis\" or fever as SpecificDisease, look questionable since they read more like disease-class usages, so I want to flag that ambiguity rather than treat the gold as fully reliable.\n\nNow I'll structure the final answer as a single Pattern Insight in a Markdown list, with the required fields: Pattern Name, Confusion Trigger, Contrastive Evidence, and Linguistic Rule, keeping any supporting analysis brief before presenting it.\n\nLooking closer at the contrast across examples, I'm testing whether the trigger is \"a generic pathophysiological/symptom head noun modified by a descriptive adjective\" — this fits telangiectasis and vasculitis cases, but fever breaks the pattern since it's a bare symptom noun without a qualifier, so I need a separate explanation for why fever still gets flagged.\n\nMy refined hypothesis: the real trigger is that the head noun belongs to a pathology/symptom lexical category (telangiectasis, vasculitis, infection, fever), which the schema's DiseaseClass category is used for in true positives (mental retardation, exudative retinal detachment). Gold labels these SpecificDisease when they function as named clinical entities rather than findings, while true positives labeled SpecificDisease tend to have proper-name or eponym/acronym heads like Coats disease or Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome.\n\nChecking syntactic role as a factor: \"ovarian cancer\" when used attributively (as in \"ovarian cancer cases\") maps to Modifier in the gold data, not DiseaseClass, which doesn't match my discrepancy cases — those are all in head/referential position (retinal telangiectasis as head of \"so-called retinal telangiectasis,\" chronic neisserial infection as sentence subject, cutaneous vasculitis as object). So I'm narrowing in on referential head position versus attributive modifier position as a distinguishing feature, alongside examples like \"fragile X syndrome\" for comparison.\n\nLooking at named entities like \"SLS\" (Sjogren-Larsson syndrome) as heads correctly labeled SpecificDisease, I'm refining the hypothesis: the real distinction might be whether the phrase functions as a proper nosological name versus a descriptive phenotypic label — but since the discrepancies (telangiectasis, vasculitis, infection, fever) are descriptive and still gold-labeled SpecificDisease, the actual rule seems to hinge on whether the descriptive label is being used as the disease's formal alias/diagnosis, as with the \"so-called\" framing in the Coats disease example.\n\nSo I'm naming this pattern: Descriptive Pathology Label Used as Disease Name. The trigger is generic pathology/symptom head nouns with anatomical or descriptive modifiers that the model misreads as category findings (DiseaseClass) rather than named diagnoses, contrasted against true SpecificDisease examples with eponymous or acronym heads, and true DiseaseClass examples that are bare phenotypic findings listed as symptoms rather than diagnostic labels.\n\nI'm trying to distill this into a single boolean test — whether a span co-refers with a named disease via parenthetical, \"so-called\", or appositive cues (→ SpecificDisease) versus functioning as a listed feature or disease category (→ DiseaseClass). But testing it against \"fever\" in example 3 breaks the rule, since fever is listed as a presenting symptom yet gold tags it as SpecificDisease, so I'm flagging this as possible annotation inconsistency rather than a clean signal.\n\nChecking the differential-diagnosis examples (chronic neisserial infection, cutaneous vasculitis), these also fit the \"named nosological entity being diagnosed or distinguished\" pattern, supporting SpecificDisease. So I'm converging on a refined rule: lexical shape (pathology noun plus modifier) is a necessary trigger but not sufficient — the real discriminator is whether the span functions referentially as a disease entity (aliased, diagnosed, or differentiated) versus describing a finding, feature, or category. I'll write this up as the final Pattern Insight, with the fever case called out as likely noise rather than core evidence, and format the output as a markdown list with contrast, trigger, evidence, and the proposed rule.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "**Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis**\n\n- **Contrast:** The gold SpecificDisease TP mentions (*Coats disease*, *Norrie disease*, *SLS*) are proper-named or acronym nosological entities that stand alone as disease names. The TP DiseaseClass mentions (*mental retardation*, *exudative retinal detachment*) are findings listed as features or consequences. The discrepancy spans are generic pathology or symptom nouns (*telangiectasis*, *vasculitis*, *infection*, *fever*) that the model reads as findings or categories.\n\n- **Isolate Trigger:** The model appears to classify by surface shape. A generic pathology or symptom head noun, often with an anatomical or descriptive premodifier (*retinal*, *cutaneous*, *unilateral*, *chronic neisserial*), looks like a finding or category label, so it defaults to DiseaseClass.\n\n- **Hypothesis Check:**\n  - **Ex 1 and 2:** *unilateral retinal telangiectasis* sits in parentheses restating \"Coats' disease,\" and *retinal telangiectasis* is introduced as \"so-called.\" These are alias signals, so the descriptive label names the disease. Gold is correct.\n  - **Ex 4 and 5:** *Chronic neisserial infection* and *cutaneous vasculitis* are the diagnoses under discussion (\"can be associated with,\" \"must be distinguished from other causes of\"). They function as nosological entities in a differential.\n  - **Ex 3:** *fever* has no premodifier, so the trigger here is the bare symptom noun alone. Its gold label is questionable, since a presenting symptom is more consistent with the *mental retardation* DiseaseClass pattern. I treat it as possible annotation noise rather than the core pattern.\n  - **Refinement:** Lexical class is necessary but not sufficient. *Ovarian cancer* has a generic head but is labeled Modifier in the TPs because it is used attributively. The real discriminator is referential: does the span name a disease that is aliased, diagnosed, or discussed as a condition, or does it name a finding or category?\n\n**Step 2: Pattern Formulation**\n\n- **Pattern Name:** Generic Pathology Noun Used as a Disease Alias or Diagnostic Label\n- **Confusion Trigger:** Generic pathology or symptom head nouns (*telangiectasis*, *vasculitis*, *infection*, *fever*), often with anatomical or descriptive premodifiers (*retinal*, *cutaneous*, *unilateral*, *chronic neisserial*). The model maps this surface form to DiseaseClass, its default label for findings.\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** SpecificDisease TPs are named nosological units (eponyms and acronyms) that work as disease names on their own. DiseaseClass TPs are findings embedded in feature lists or consequences. The TP descriptive phrase *ovarian cancer* is attributive and labeled Modifier, which shows that a descriptive form alone should not determine the class.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Label a pathology or symptom-noun span as **SpecificDisease** when it refers to a nosological entity that is named, aliased, or diagnosed in context. This applies when (a) it is co-referent with a named disease through parentheses, an appositive, \"so-called,\" or \"this condition,\" or (b) it is the subject or object of a diagnostic or etiologic predicate (\"presents with,\" \"associated with,\" \"distinguished from,\" \"causes of\"). Keep **DiseaseClass** when the span denotes an enumerated feature or finding with no individual disease referent. Treat descriptor-plus-pathology-noun shape only as a candidate trigger for review, never as the decision criterion.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4291, "output_tokens": 10258, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 8991}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}